{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat /input/INPUT.md'","aggregated_output":"# Audit input\n\n## Exposition\n\nFor a real pair (u,v), let p=|u| and q=|u|+|v|. Since p<=q and |v|<=q, the output |v| is bounded by p.\n\n## Supplied formal proposition and argument\n\nThe formal result proves p<=q and |v|<=q, using nonnegativity of absolute values.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"verdict\": \"discrepancy\",\n  \"statementAssessment\": \"The source’s concluding bound is false for real pairs in general. The supplied formal result proves two true bounds, but neither is the claimed bound.\",\n  \"methodAssessment\": \"The formal argument uses nonnegativity of absolute values to prove p ≤ q and |v| ≤ q. Those steps match the corresponding claims in the exposition. The inference from them to |v| ≤ p is unsupported.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"sourceAssertion\": \"For p = |u| and q = |u| + |v|, “the output |v| is bounded by p.”\",\n      \"formalCounterpart\": \"The formal result proves p ≤ q and |v| ≤ q; it does not prove |v| ≤ p.\",\n      \"classification\": \"false statement\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The claimed bound does not hold for every real pair. The bounds involving q do not establish a bound by p.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Take u = 0 and v = 1. Then p = 0 and |v| = 1, so |v| ≤ p would say 1 ≤ 0.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"None.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"sourceAssertion\": \"“Since p ≤ q and |v| ≤ q,” conclude |v| ≤ p.\",\n      \"formalCounterpart\": \"The formal result establishes p ≤ q and |v| ≤ q.\",\n      \"classification\": \"unsupported implication\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Both quantities being bounded above by q gives no ordering between |v| and p. The two formal bounds faithfully correspond to the stated intermediate inequalities, but they do not justify the exposition’s conclusion.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"For u = 0 and v = 1, p ≤ q and |v| ≤ q both hold (0 ≤ 1 and 1 ≤ 1), while |v| ≤ p fails.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"None.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"examinedAssertions\": [\n    {\n      \"assertion\": \"“For a real pair (u,v), let p=|u| and q=|u|+|v|.”\",\n      \"assessment\": \"Definition in the exposition; the formal summary uses the same p and q notation.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The supplied formal proposition and argument refer to p and q and to the same absolute-value expressions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"assertion\": \"“Since p≤q and |v|≤q”\",\n      \"assessment\": \"Both inequalities are true and are exactly the two claims the supplied formal result proves.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Since |v| ≥ 0, p = |u| ≤ |u| + |v| = q; since |u| ≥ 0, |v| ≤ |u| + |v| = q.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"assertion\": \"“the output |v| is bounded by p.”\",\n      \"assessment\": \"False in general.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"At (u,v) = (0,1), |v| = 1 and p = 0.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"omissions\": \"The supplied formal result omits the exposition’s desired conclusion |v| ≤ p.\",\n  \"toolsUsed\": \"Read /input/INPUT.md with cat; used direct mathematical reasoning.\",\n  \"checkingStatus\": \"No Lean checks were run. The input provides a rendered formal argument, not an original Lean module.\"\n}"}}
